    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "It's still a bit too \"safe\" and corporate. I feel like I'm repeating myself, but I really need to capture that specific feeling of something being *wrong* in a way that logic can't explain. \n\nWhen I say \"irrational behavior\" or \"unseen disruption,\" I'm looking for a way to describe it that isn't just a software bug. I want to convey that it feels like there's this tiny, almost subtle force at work that is just messing with the system—something that behaves with a sort of strange, unpredictable autonomy. \n\nCould you try to use a metaphor in that paragraph? I want a way to describe these errors that suggests they come from a source that is fundamentally outside of our technical understanding—some kind of invisible, persistent, and slightly chaotic influence that just disrupts the order of the warehouse. Something that makes the error feel less like a math problem and more like a weird, inexplicable event.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
